{
  "title": "MedRAX \u2014 healthcare (1,173\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: MedRAX\nDomain: healthcare | Scale: medium | Stars: 1,173\nProblem: Specialized chest X-ray (CXR) analysis models operate in isolation, limiting their practical utility in clinical settings. MedRAX unifies state-of-the-art CXR analysis tools and multimodal LLMs into a single AI agent capable of addressing complex medical queries without additional training.\nTarget users: Clinical radiologists, Medical AI researchers, Healthcare institutions deploying automated CXR interpretation, AI/ML researchers benchmarking medical reasoning agents\nCore features: Agentic multi-tool orchestration for CXR analysis using LangChain/LangGraph; Disease classification detecting 18 pathology classes via DenseNet-121 (TorchXRayVision); Anatomical segmentation using MedSAM and PSPNet; Automated radiology report generation via SwinV2 Transformer; Visual QA and medical reasoning via CheXagent and LLaVA-Med; Phrase grounding and finding localization via Maira-2; Synthetic CXR generation using RoentGen diffusion model; ChestAgentBench evaluation framework with 2,500 complex medical queries\nIntegrations: OpenAI GPT-4o (backbone LLM with vision), Anthropic Claude (via langchain-anthropic), Hugging Face Transformers and model hub, Gradio (web interface), DICOM processing libraries\nTech stack: Gradio + Python, FastAPI, Uvicorn + CUDA/GPU, local or cloud-based model weight storage\nKey libraries: langchain-core, langchain-community, langchain-openai, langgraph, torchxrayvision, transformers, torch, torchvision, diffusers, timm, peft, bitsandbytes, accelerate, opencv-python, pydicom, scikit-image, albumentations\nArchitecture: event-driven\nDesign lessons:\n- Tool-agnostic modular design allows new CXR analysis models to be plugged in or removed without core agent changes, enabling incremental capability expansion.\n- Using LangGraph for agent orchestration enables dynamic, multi-step reasoning loops where the LLM selects and chains specialized tools at inference time without task-specific fine-tuning.\n- Supporting 4-bit and 8-bit quantization (via bitsandbytes) for large vision-language models (LLaVA-Med, Maira-2) is critical for practical GPU memory constraints in clinical deployments.\n- Introducing a dedicated benchmark (ChestAgentBench) alongside the system provides reproducible, structured evaluation across diverse CXR reasoning categories, a pattern increasingly important for agentic medical AI.",
